WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.179
This would have been a talk that I gave at
a live show with an audience,

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:05.160
but it's June 2020 as I record this,

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.860
so instead, I am at home, talking to my camera,

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.615
and possibly my neighbours
if they can hear me,

00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:12.869
and also cursing the drivers with loud engines
who seem intent

00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:16.760
on making as much noise as possible directly
outside my flat today.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.629
I asked 64,000 people about the children's rhyme
"Jingle Bells, Batman Smells".

00:00:21.629 --> 00:00:23.388
And here is what I found out.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.920
When I was a kid, the next line of that
was "Robin flew away".

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.839
That was how the song went in my school.
No more lines.

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:34.039
Turns out there are multiple completing versions,
including the one that

00:00:34.039 --> 00:00:38.390
singer and actor Robert Goulet sang
on the Simpsons in December 1993.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.414
That version goes, "Jingle Bells, Batman smells..."

00:00:47.652 --> 00:00:51.500
"Flew away" versus "laid an egg".
Two different bird jokes.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.519
And here's the hypothesis that I want to test:

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:57.540
that the Simpsons measurably changed which
version of that song was popular.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.079
That kids who saw that episode,
either when it first aired or in reruns,

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:03.590
either learned it for the
first time from there,

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.350
or it replaced the version they knew.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.500
Because, according to various pop histories,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.110
that song was around for decades
before the Simpsons.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.670
It probably started in California in the 60s,

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.140
inspired by the Adam West Batman TV series,
then it was spread by military kids

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.310
moving around America and the world
with their families. Probably.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.620
There are other Jingle Bells parodies,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.860
including some really genuinely awful
bigoted ones,

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.110
I am not considering them.
I'm asking, very specifically,

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.240
about the one where Batman smells.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.540
Never mind that it's entirely
the wrong part of the year.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.412
It's 2020. Time is meaningless.
Let's talk about Jingle Bells in the middle of summer.

00:01:38.412 --> 00:01:39.830
So. I sent out a survey.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.159
First, I asked each person for
two bits of information:

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.503
the year they were born, and the country
they were in when they were eight.

00:01:46.503 --> 00:01:48.871
I figure eight years old is about
the right time for a kid

00:01:48.871 --> 00:01:52.150
to be endlessly singing this at their friends
in the playground.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.730
Now, if the person answering said they were
from the US or UK,

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.729
I asked for their zipcode,
or the first part of their postcode,

00:01:58.729 --> 00:01:59.810
from when they were a kid.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.149
That meant I could very roughly put a pin
in a map for each of them.

00:02:04.149 --> 00:02:07.380
And there are those pins, thank you very much
to everyone who responded.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.650
And then, I asked two questions:

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:13.180
first, what they called the
most basic playground chase game,

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.490
what Americans call "tag", and then second,

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.670
to complete the song:
Jingle Bells, Batman Smells.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.150
For both those questions,
I didn't offer any suggestions,

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.660
I didn't want to prompt with any words that
might make people go

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.630
"oh, yeah, sure, that one I guess?"

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.780
They had to remember.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.140
Which was probably a good idea,

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.640
but it meant that sifting through this data
was a nightmare,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.110
because give people a blank text box

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.210
and some of them will decide to tell you their
life story in it.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:42.540
In the analysis, I only included respondents
who used the word "Robin" in their answer.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.200
That's because for the first couple of hours
of that survey being out,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.410
I hadn't made it clear that you could just
leave that answer blank if you didn't know.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.240
So quite a lot of people who didn't know the song
interpreted the question as

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.200
"make up some lyrics of your own".
That's my bad.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.260
I suspect a few people back then
also Googled the answer,

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.840
but I don't think that'll have affected the
final results all that much.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.300
That also removed people who were just quoting
either Spongebob Squarepants or Madagascar.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:12.060
Those versions were just invented by scriptwriters
and forced into kids' heads by marketing.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.600
I don't want to count those here.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.730
I was going to filter out anyone who didn't
use either "flew" or "egg",

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:23.210
but it turns out more than 1,000 people know the
next line of the song as "Robin ran away"?

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:28.490
I think if you don't have a bird pun in that line,
you're missing out, but who am I to judge?

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:32.520
I also filtered anything that was obviously
spam or malicious.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.470
When all that was said and done,
once all that filtering was finished,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:43.490
I had answers from 42,886 people who knew
some version of the song with "Robin".

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:46.700
Here's the breakdown by year of birth,
and by geography.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.430
Now, while I'm still counting answers from
earlier years,

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.540
I'm only going to show graphs from
1973 and onwards,

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.050
because those are the birth 
where I have more than 50 replies

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.460
from both the US and UK.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.050
The graphs get weird before then,
and I think that's because

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.210
the number of people who put in
false birth years

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.310
starts to have a really significant effect.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.780
I also can't collect data from anyone born
in 2007 or later,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.340
because the law's very strict on collecting
data from kids under 13.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.049
So with all that in place, I tracked keywords
for the various versions of the song.

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:19.390
If you had a completely unique version,
I wasn't going to count it,

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.120
because it might contain personal information.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.600
Shoutout to the one person who was insulting
their siblings in their answer,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.080
at great lengths, and also explained all the jokes.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.700
But if a few people had roughly the same version,
I did include a check for it.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.300
So. Let's talk about those many other variants.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.030
I'm not musical, and frankly, singing a half-dozen
ridiculous versions of Jingle Bells

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.140
sounds like something that will
very much come back to haunt me,

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.382
but I do know someone who can perform these
far better than I can.

00:04:45.382 --> 00:04:46.382
Jack.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.730
That version was only sent in by a few Australians,

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.020
but also two people from
north-west Washington DC.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.704
So maybe they were diplomatic or army kids?
No idea.

00:05:10.704 --> 00:05:12.270
Oh, and in other versions of that,

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.720
it's Donald Duck and Mickey Mouse
doing the stabbing.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.271
Because children are monsters.

00:05:17.271 --> 00:05:20.360
And on that note,
let's cover the thirty-nine people,

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.850
again, from all over the world,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.017
who all share this second verse.

00:05:39.532 --> 00:05:42.050
That was one of the less violent
versions of it,

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.440
one ends with a request to be
shot in the head.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.850
Now, like I said, I did rule out any answer
that didn't include Robin,

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.140
but I do want to highlight just one
international version of Jingle Bells,

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.660
just to show that this is not entirely
an English-speaking joke.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.040
Sweden has many variations on this:

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:11.480
The colour and particular private part of the bear
that's painted appear to change between people.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:15.410
Now we get to the most common versions.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:18.690
One is almost exclusive to the UK
and New Zealand,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.760
because those are the countries that use the word
"motorway" for big highways.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.680
More than two thousand people
have some variation on this:

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:35.150
There are a long cast of characters,

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.540
all of whom lost some usually-intimate part
of their body or clothing,

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:43.330
on various motorways that change depending
on where in the UK you are.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:46.089
In south Wales, it's "on the M4 motorway".

00:06:46.089 --> 00:06:47.628
In Scotland, the M9.

00:06:47.628 --> 00:06:52.085
62 people, almost all Australians, said the
last line of that should be:

00:06:56.095 --> 00:06:58.650
TAA being a now-defunct Australian airline.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:02.670
However, that is better than the two Scottish people
who said it should be:

00:07:07.432 --> 00:07:11.600
And the idea of 90s kids-TV
fever-nightmare Mr Blobby

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.570
just defecating in the middle of the--

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.045
--I can't say that!

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:20.674
Then there's this one from 221 mostly-British
people, where after Robin flew away:

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.676
And then from 23 British people,
mostly 80s kids:

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:35.834
Kojak, fictional TV detective with a lollipop
part of pop culture in the UK in the 80s.

00:07:40.746 --> 00:07:43.673
Again, British, more than 100 people.

00:07:43.673 --> 00:07:45.783
Version after version after version of the
song came up

00:07:45.783 --> 00:07:50.150
as being popular over here but not in the
US or Canada.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:53.090
There was only one significant variant
I could find

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.832
that was definitely North American:

00:07:59.351 --> 00:08:02.050
More than 1500 people know that version,

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:05.510
including more than 15% of the
Canadians who replied.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.270
Almost no-one outside North America
has heard it.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:10.468
There were a couple of smaller American variations.

00:08:18.546 --> 00:08:21.180
90 people, with a big cluster
in the Pacific Northwest.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.040
The exact year changes, but almost everyone
in that group agreed

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.239
that Robin "laid" the gun.

00:08:27.187 --> 00:08:29.234
And the final North American variant is:

00:08:31.803 --> 00:08:33.919
Which just seems a bit half-assed, frankly,

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:35.510
like it got made up by a parent

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:37.936
trying to give the kids something
more wholesome to sing?

00:08:37.936 --> 00:08:40.017
But it was only 35 people.

00:08:40.017 --> 00:08:43.590
America seems to have way fewer regional variations
than the UK,

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.500
and I have no idea why.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:47.240
That doesn't just happen for songs, either.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.680
In this book from 1959,
"The Lore and Language of Schoolchildren",

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.600
Iona and Peter Opie conducted a survey of
dozens of schools in Britain,

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:57.910
asking about the words and rhymes they use.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:59.560
It is a famous piece of research,

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:02.930
but it's also a catalogue on how
children are really cruel to each other.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.470
There's a whole chapter in here on
nicknames and epithets,

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.140
and I'll just read from the
table of contents here:

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:11.100
they have a list of names for
Spoil-sports, Sourpusses, Spitfires,

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.070
Clever-dicks, Dafties, Fools, Dunces,
Copycats, Gibbers, Cheats, Swankpots,

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:20.630
Nosey Parkers, Stare-cats, Cowards,
Crybabies, Sneaks, Crawlers, Bullies.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:23.670
Plus, there's a whole section on
"tortures and hair-pulling",

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:26.647
and a separate chapter on
physical appearance insults.

00:09:26.647 --> 00:09:28.670
Children are monsters.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.190
Anyway, what this book also has are maps.

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:35.470
They're the best known bit: this old copy here
actually has its spine broken

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.580
on one of the map pages,
presumably because someone photocopied it.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:42.089
This map covers "truce terms", the words that
kids use to say that they are

00:09:42.089 --> 00:09:44.970
temporarily out of a game,
because they've got stitch,

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:46.380
or they're tying their shoelaces.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.080
In the UK, those words change not just in
different parts of the country,

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.040
but even from one town to the next.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:55.529
And that's why I asked about the game that
people call "tag".

00:09:55.529 --> 00:09:57.730
Because if you're American,
of course it's called tag,

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:02.250
more than 96% of Americans and Canadians replying
said it was called tag,

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.110
and almost everyone who disagreed misidentified
a different game

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.700
and wrote something like "hide and seek",

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:10.010
maybe because they didn't think I'd be asking
such a simple question.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.293
In America: it's. called. tag.

00:10:13.293 --> 00:10:16.700
In Britain, only 29% of people
called it tag.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:19.910
Not only are there different names,
but just like the Opies did for truce terms,

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.431
you can draw a map of what
"tag" is called around the UK.

00:10:23.431 --> 00:10:27.370
For some reason, America tends to be one massive,
homogenous blob,

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:30.010
even for games and songs that are made up
more recently,

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.810
whereas Britain has loads of variants.

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.390
Which brings me, finally, to the big question.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:38.250
Is it "laid an egg" or "flew away"?

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.210
And did the Simpsons change it?

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.570
So first of all, let's compare the basic numbers.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:47.670
Now, all I'm doing here is showing the percentage
of people who said "laid an egg"

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.330
out of all the replies who knew
some version of the song.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.250
And you can see, "egg",
by far the most popular.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.730
78% said it's "egg".
So it seems like there's a clear winner:

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:00.250
until you break it down by country.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.980
More than 98% of Americans
said it was "laid an egg".

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.120
98% percent! You can't get 98% of Americans
to agree on anything.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.190
Of course the Simpsons chose it.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:14.220
Only someone who grew up outside the US would
ever think that sounds wrong.

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:17.910
But "Robin flew away" is clearly
the British version.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:21.240
But remember, the answers were also
broken down by year of birth.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.890
For the US, it's always a very high percentage,
of course.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:28.420
But if the Simpsons had an effect in the UK,
then we'd expect to see that 90s kids,

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:31.380
which means people born from the early eighties
onwards,

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.195
they would be more likely
to know the "egg" version.

00:11:35.923 --> 00:11:39.390
And I wish, I wish, I wish, I had a camera
on my face when I first saw that data.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:42.050
I could not ask for better proof of that hypothesis.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:44.710
Can I 100% trace that back to the Simpsons?

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:46.580
No, of course not,
there could be some other factor.

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:51.080
But the Simpsons was one of the most important
pop-culture shows in the UK in the mid-90s,

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:53.410
a lot of folks will have seen that episode,

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:56.470
and I cannot think of any other reason for
that change.

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:00.420
And it also explains why the change happens
everywhere in the UK at once:

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.990
this isn't a local version that spread outward
from a single location,

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:06.350
it's a version that appeared everywhere,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:08.140
probably because it was imposed by television.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:10.910
And as the Simpsons drifts into history,

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.000
as kids aren't watching it any more,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.260
the British versions of the song
are reasserting themselves.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:21.970
Yes, America, Robin might well lay an egg for you,
but over here? He flies away.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:23.860
- Thank you, Jack.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:25.524
- Thanks! Today's sponsor is...

00:12:25.524 --> 00:12:26.670
- No!

